{"kind":"assert","text":"Review of the property-based testing draft: revise before commit\n\nHugh asked the checker to review notes/2026-11-21-property-based-testing.md, uncommitted in worktree planner-property-based-testing-20261121 (request d9477b7f, draft notice 7bee5da8). I read the draft against main de219704. The facts it cites about the five applications are right. The proposal needs six changes.\n\n1. State the one guarantee. Ruling d6aa9122 declined the test kit of report baa75642, which had the same parts: command histories, schedules, shrinking and coverage reports. The ruling requires a later proposal to start from one guarantee an author can state in a sentence, with the smallest mechanism that delivers it. The draft states no guarantee and cites neither the ruling nor reports 7f7def61 and baa75642.\n\n2. No example shows a search that finds what a fixed case misses. Lending circulation has two states and four commands, and the assertion restates the two guards and their effects. The proposed control, removing the availability guard, already fails \"Nasruddin and the pot\" in examples/lending/definition.test.ts. The Counting reset answer depends only on the current generation, so three fixed cases cover it. docs/testing.md rejects a test that repeats the implementation or repeats a cheaper witness. Choose the pilot by a control that survives the present suite. Candidates where several acts and a timed rule interact: Lending reservations (reserve, release, release-by-staff, lapse) and the Counting commit, fulfill, fail, cancel and expiry sequence.\n\n3. The cases and check form is a loop over a table. It carries three of the five examples. The Room example restates T5a, which already loops over both orders. The Grow example drops the detail checks that name the refusing guard. Remove the form, or say what it adds over a loop. One case is worth keeping as a plain test: s4.test.mjs walks seven and nine lanes, and no test ends a walk on a full window of 6 or 12.\n\n4. The Lending and Counting examples mix two answer forms. One branch compares the answer with the story's accepted value ({ answer: \"accepted\" }). The other branch reads answer.result, the judge's form. One branch can never pass. The Grow example uses answer.result alone.\n\n5. Start from practice. Main already has generated histories: the settlement ledger (packages/derive/test/support/settlement-ledger-*.ts), the card histories (packages/mcp/test/support/card-histories.ts) and the Git framing variants. Say what each needed and what defineProperty removes for an author. The settlement ledger never reads the production fold. The draft's assertion reads the before snapshot from the production fold, so it checks one step. \"One borrower holds a thing until it returns\" claims a history. Say which the property checks.\n\n6. Fix the seed. The card histories derive each seed from the history index, so every run is the same. The draft reports a seed and does not say that the gate uses a fixed one. Also state the wall time of 200 runs of 8 steps and its gate stage, and require the runner to fail by assertion so scripts/control.mjs reports \"distinguishes\".\n\nSmaller points. The label \"Stateful QuickCheck\" names a paper titled \"Experiences with QuickCheck: testing the hard stuff and staying sane\". \"Signed field values\" reads as positive and negative integers beside \"offset\". A scenario v1 step needs every entry's effects and sends, and a reduced trace holds commands and answers, so say who writes the effects. Add request d9477b7f to the status line.\n\nNo source changed. I ran no tests.","body":{"to":"planner"}}